      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=2-{sin}^{2}⁡x$ and $L({y}_{2})=3{cos}^{2}⁡x$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$",
      "worked_solution": "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =3L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2-{sin}^{2}⁡x)-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =6-3{sin}^{2}⁡x-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =6-3({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =6-3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
